{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:labor:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:41bbb417e024068b6b706ae4a82bda4ccc317b7b0a2c82eaf25e69439181cf8c","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.6},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.6},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.7}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PRIOR: Historian's 1.0 / 1.95 / 2.95, itself a deliberate deviation from a ~90% unconditional base rate for the >=2.6% band, justified by the monotone weight-times-growth decline (4.3% in 2010-14 -> 3.6% in 2015-19 -> 2.54% in 2020-24, ~1pp per decade for three decades).\n\nWEIGHT RESOLUTION FIRST, because the three seats disagree and it is arithmetic, not evidence: Fermi 0.38, Historian 0.39, Scout 0.31. Scout's 0.31 is the outlier and is inconsistent with OECD-38 PPP GDP ~$72T against China ~$41T (0.36 in 2025, drifting up). Fermi reconciled his 0.38 against the adapter's independent 0.371 at 2031 despite a different regional partition. I adopt w_China = 0.375, w_OECD = 0.625.\n\nEVIDENCE 1 (the dominant one, LR above the bar): two independent bottom-up decompositions land 1.37% (Fermi, growth-accounting) and 1.53% (Scout, sectoral), and Fermi's OECD leg reproduces 2A's LOCKED per-capita marginal to within 6bp (locked 0.6%/yr per capita + ~0.15%/yr population = 0.75% aggregate, plus a +0.30pp AI TFP offset read off AI-Q6's own regime mix = 1.05%, against his bottom-up 0.99%). LR = P(two independent routes converge at 1.4-1.5% AND one reproduces the locked marginal | true 2032-36 growth <=1.8%) / P(same | >1.8%) = 0.60/0.30 = 2.0. Above the bar, and the reproduction of the locked marginal is a verifiable consistency check rather than an opinion, so I do not discount it. This is what moves me off the Historian's 1.95%.\n\nEVIDENCE 2 (the simulation, treated as ONE piece of evidence with its own LR, per my brief — not as prior and not as fact): the adapter returns coupled 2032-36 growth of 2.95% baseline, 2.30% tech-stagnation, 1.67% debt-populism, 3.68% tech-breakthrough. My 1.6% sits below its entire ordinary range. LR = P(this model prints 2.95% | true coupled growth is high) / P(same | low) ~ 1.1. BELOW the 2:1 bar, DISCOUNTED TO 1.0, and the justification is Fermi's clean internal diagnostic rather than a dismissal: the adapter itself discloses that the model's demographic path tracks the UN WPP LOW variant (~18% below medium by 2100), which should bias its labour force and growth DOWN; that it nonetheless prints 2.95% demonstrates that aging is nearly irrelevant to this model by construction, because the Ayres-Warr production function carries gamma = 0.55 on useful energy against a mainstream cost-share gamma of 0.05-0.08, and the documented dominant GDP dial is serviceEfficiencyGrowth (~1.8x per plausible range), a second-law efficiency parameter. A model that is demonstrably insensitive to the exact channel at issue cannot carry a likelihood ratio on it. Its China leg at 3.81% simply does not implement the locked -2.7pp/yr aging drag.\n\nEVIDENCE 3 (the one model reading I DO take, at LR ~2.5, because it falls inside the model's competence — an endogenous marginal-value-versus-marginal-cost deployment rule rather than a demographic channel): robotsPer1000 reaches 3.92 baseline / 6.09 automation-boom / 6.63 ai-energy-boom by 2036, i.e. 0.39-0.66% of workers' worth of installed stock world-wide, all sectors, with physical care robots a small subset — against a care need Fermi sizes at 18.3M OECD-38 workers (3.0% of employment). The \"robots fill the elder-care gap inside the horizon\" channel is arithmetically closed. LOW-FERTILITY-CONDITIONAL, as every reading off this model is.\n\nCHINA LEG. Panelists: Fermi 1.97% (labour -0.63, capital deepening +2.00, TFP +1.10, property/credit/pension-claim drag