back to a locked marginal.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:55.760Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD or national administrative earnings data show cumulative real median hourly wage growth in ISIC F + Q exceeding ISIC J + K + M by >= 4.0 percentage points over 2025-2030 — i.e. on pace for the 10.0pp CQ1 threshold — with the gap visible in a national statistical office series (not a job board) and NOT attributable to F+Q composition shifting toward physicians and skilled trades.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ1 conjunct B at 0.105 is wrong and the care shortfall is clearing by PRICE rather than by rationing. CQ1 moves from 9 to 22-26. Hypothesis plank (a) moves from 5 to ~18. CQ6-C moves up ~6 points (the fiscal transfer is being made, so the Baumol inflation channel is live) with CQ6-B down by the same. This is the most direct falsifier of my whole effort, because the 0.60 likelihood ratio I applied to the NSI vacancy-easing signal is the single largest discount in my CQ1 arithmetic.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China's annualized real GDP growth in constant 2025 USD PPP averages >= 4.5%/yr over 2028-2031 on IMF WEO or World Bank data, with no reclassification of the PPP benchmark explaining it.\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The locked -2.7pp/yr aging drag is not binding as I priced it, and the weight-times-drag product (-1.03pp of coupled growth) that carries my whole CQ3 update collapses. CQ3 P50 moves up +0.5pp to ~2.1%, P90 to ~3.1%. CQ6-A moves from 11 to ~22 and CQ6-D from 15 to ~9. Hypothesis plank (d) moves from 13 to ~25. Note this would also partially vindicate the simulation's China leg of 3.81%, which I discounted to LR 1.0 — so it falsifies my handling of the model evidence, not just my China number.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat / OECD AI-use surveys show the unweighted share of OECD-38 firms with >= 10 employees using AI directly in producing goods or services passing 35% — i.e. clearly on track for the AI-Q2 50% threshold — while PPP-weighted OECD TFP growth over the same period remains below +0.30pp/yr above the 2015-2022 baseline.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This CONFIRMS my scissors (adoption up, measured TFP flat) rather than falsifying it, so the falsifying branch is the mirror: if adoption is still BELOW 20% by end-2030, then labour scarcity is NOT the adoption driver I claim and my upward revision of AI-Q2 to ~30 is wrong, which removes the demand-pull leg of my coupling finding and pushes AI-Q1 down further on diffusion grounds instead of composition grounds. Either reading moves CQ6-B's composition, not its size. If instead BOTH adoption passes 35% AND TFP clears +0.50pp/yr, the wedge itself is false: CQ3 P50 up +0.4pp, CQ6-A to ~20, and my CQ6-B residual falls to ~42.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The share of newly licensed US RNs aged 40 and over rises above 15% (from the historical ~6-8%) in NCSBN or state board of nursing licensure data, OR total annual new RN + LPN licensures rise more than 25% above their 2025 level, in either case without a reduction in clinical placement requirements.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The stock-flow mismatch — the single mechanism behind my hypothesis plank (a) at 5 and a large part of CQ1's conjunct B — is clearing. The ~7% coverage arithmetic (0.12M/yr convertible against a 1.66M/yr need) would be wrong by a factor of two or more. Plank (a) moves from 5 to ~22; CQ1 to ~16; CQ6-C up ~4 as the care supply wall softens. This is a licensing-queue falsifier specifically, and it is the cleanest one, because licensure counts are published, dated and administrative — unlike the MOVE series I had to refuse.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"In the next MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >= 15%, G7 GDP-weighted 10-year nominal sovereign yields RISE by >= 25bp during the drawdown window (rather than falling >= 50bp), reported on benchmark closes.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My 0.36 conditional for P(bonds fail to hedge | drawdown) — shaded DOWN from the historical 3-of-8 = 0.38 on the argument that the modal drawdown here is a disinflationary AI capex bust — is wrong, and the regime has already switched. CQ5 moves from 18 to ~28 (conjunct B from 0.38 to ~0.55, carrying conjunct A up through the +0.45 correlation). CQ4's P90 moves from 3.3 to ~3.6 and the P50 up ~15bp, since the same regime is the eroding-convenience-yield/term-premium regime. CQ6-C up ~3 points. Conversely, a >= 25% drawdown in which G7 nominals fall >= 100bp would confirm my shade and take CQ5 to ~13.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 2 bayes seat, effort 'labor'."}}